186045417] [17592186135923 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135923 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "93e6ba35-344d-5675-8ced-062323c83944" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135924 #edb/ref 1080 "2d + 5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135924 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135924 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a49bd6f6-2e96-5aa1-9e59-2350f885e2c0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135925 #edb/ref 1080 "6d + 3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135925 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135925 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "de69d80d-2fce-5c76-a458-cbe80f054cf1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135926 #edb/ref 1080 "3d + 3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135926 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135926 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2341be70-1972-5001-b5d2-6fa5f432827e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135927 #edb/ref 1080 "3d + 6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135927 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135927 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "7653b9e2-65df-5de6-b25a-b214dbedff6f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138282 #edb/ref 1069 "e-2565" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138282 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $C$, $D$ and $E$ be three collinear points such that $D$ is between $C$ and $E$. If $CE = 8\\,\\text{cm}$ and $CD = 6\\,\\text{cm}$, find the length of $\\overline{DE}$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138282 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5b1ee9ef-da39-5f7a-abb5-98149a765b1f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138282 #edb/ref 1074 "![](images/30/304a0de1777efb414a2504e22d53c1d15a5a0299196a7a9103a02491ff12e4c6.png)\n\nThe segment addition postulate states that\n\n$$\nCE = CD + DE\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known values into the formula above, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nCE &= CD + DE \\\\\n8 &= 6 + DE \\\\\n8 - 6 &= DE \\\\\n2 &= DE\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nDE = 2 \\, \\text{cm}\n$$\n\n." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138283 #edb/ref 1069 "e-2566" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138283 #edb/ref 1071 "Given the diagram shown below, what is the measure of $\\overline{BC}$?\n\n![](images/54/543c7c26c7d56d8ff10f4ece71b74bb5f04edf59fddb6f2c0df549848000abaf.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138283 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cabd7daa-4f22-56ca-8b26-0791857e8eae" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138283 #edb/ref 1074 "The segment addition postulate can be applied to a set of more than three collinear points. In the given situation, it states that\n\n$$\nAD = AB + BC +CD\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known values into the equation above, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAD &= AB + BC + CD \\\\\n21 &= 5 + BC + 12 \\\\\n21 &= 17 + BC \\\\\n21 - 17 &= BC \\\\\nBC &= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138284 #edb/ref 1069 "e-2567" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138284 #edb/ref 1071 "Given the diagram shown below, what is the value of $x$?\n\n![](images/3f/3fe1e3d1e6f89af1050d0437379a811eabb19212be36f0fecd91ddfaca2594ee.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138284 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6df54266-d6d6-54f0-8b61-4b5eabf0fcbc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138284 #edb/ref 1074 "By the segment addition postulate, we have\n\n$$\nAD = AB + BC +CD\n$$\n\nTo solve for $x$, we can substitute the given information\n\n$$\nAD=30, \\quad AB=x+5, \\quad BC=13, \\quad CD=5x\n$$\n\nand then solve the resulting equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAD &= AB + BC + CD \\\\\n30 &= (x + 5) + 13 + 5x \\\\\n30 &= 6x + 18 \\\\\n6x &= 12 \\\\\nx &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138285 #edb/ref 1069 "e-2568" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138285 #edb/ref 1071 "In the diagram below, the length of the line segment $\\overline{AD}$ is $8d+12$, $AB = 4d+4$, and $C$ is the midpoint of $\\overline{BD}$. Express the length of $\\overline{AC}$ in terms of $d$.\n\n![](images/46/468fd25032d1a36e1658331053a33652cef7ff9ce3d9c95b729d6a7b7ebf1672.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138285 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fc35d08c-518a-5f03-8e5f-627d58c572d3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138285 #edb/ref 1074 "First, note that according to the segment addition postulate, we have\n\n$$\nAD = AB + BD\n$$\n\nwhich, in turn, gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nBD &= AD - AB \\\\\n&= (8d + 12) - (4d + 4) \\\\\n&= 4d + 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $C$ is the midpoint of $BD$, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nBC &= \\frac{1}{2}BD \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(4d + 8) \\\\\n&= 2d + 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAC &= AB + BC \\\\\n&= (4d + 4) + (2d + 4) \\\\\n&= 6d + 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138558 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048725 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138558 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050563 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138558 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186138768 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138558 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186138769 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138558 #edb/ref 1039 "Calculating the Distance Between Three Collinear Points" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138558 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186138282 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138558 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "3108f0ab-17b1-5241-93d1-97658cf22601" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138559 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186138831 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138559 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186138832 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138559 #edb/ref 1039 "Calculating the Distance Between More Than Three Collinear Points" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138559 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186138283 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138559 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "d7679cf4-427a-59ed-8b0f-d483af20ede8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138560 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048673 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138560 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050452 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138560 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186138830 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138560 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186138833 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138560 #edb/ref 1039 "Calculating the Value of an Unknown Quantity Using Collinearity" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138560 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186138284 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138560 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "c2c48d15-4749-5c47-bc97-a5584216cf2e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138561 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049496 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138561 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050476 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138561 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186138767 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138561 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186138829 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138561 #edb/ref 1039 "Calculating the Value of an Unknown Quantity Using Collinearity and Midpoints" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138561 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186138285 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138561 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "da4a8391-42b6-510c-8cb4-acbbbe7d1585" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138602 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186139556 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138602 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186139557 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138602 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186139558 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138602 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186139559 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138602 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186139560 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138602 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186139561 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138602 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138602 #edb/ref 1045 "The Segment Addition Postulate" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138602 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186139556 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138602 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050505 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138602 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "522ceb8a-6a60-5c1f-99d9-c6421e013ec3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138829 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186135922 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138829 #edb/ref 1069 "q-52726" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138829 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/46/468fd25032d1a36e1658331053a33652cef7ff9ce3d9c95b729d6a7b7ebf1672.png)\n\nIn the diagram above, the length of the line segment $AB^(―)$ is $5d + 6$, and $AD = 11d + 12$. If $C$ is the midpoint of $BD^(―)$, find the length of $CD^(―)$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138829 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "01a9d857-28c1-509b-bab9-73d8c8e051ef" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139556 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186139557 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139556 #edb/ref 1061 8720 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139556 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "11ec6bb7-b9b3-51ea-b1b2-5f6a0a7b75b9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139556 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186139635 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139557 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186139558 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139557 #edb/ref 1061 8722 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139557 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "fa4d60ee-75e2-54e2-b7eb-e1946cf89388" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139557 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186139636 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139558 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186139559 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139558 #edb/ref 1061 8723 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139558 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ab8e5676-8695-5fd6-8d78-6bae4eba68f9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139558 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186138558 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139559 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186139560 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139559 #edb/ref 1061 8724 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139559 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "df7668d0-a581-5a0f-a689-b2079711a0d2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139559 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186138559 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139560 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186139561 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139560 #edb/ref 1061 8725 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139560 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "24ee0c7c-ff8c-5b75-b742-a7f922b432ab" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139560 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186138560 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139561 #edb/ref 1061 8726 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139561 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e6dafd2a-98a4-508e-8294-b9f6ffd16d06" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139561 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186138561 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139635 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's look at the three points $A$, $B$, and $C$ below.\n\n![](images/95/956f9b2c979f06897b09119b089ced903e095d72136e64b9ea52e946fba41544.png)\n\nAs indicated in the diagram, the distances between the points are\n\n$$\nAB = 4cm, BC = 2cm, AC = 6cm\n$$\n\nCan we be sure that the points are collinear? To answer this, we use the **segment addition postulate**.\n\nThe postulate states the following:\n\n> *Three points are collinear if and only if the largest distance between two of them is equal to the sum of the other two distances.*\n\nHere, the largest distance is $AC$, so the points $A$, $B$, and $C$ are collinear *if and only if*\n\n$$\nAC=AB + BC\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given information\n\n$$\nAC=6\\,\\mathrm{cm}\n$$\n\n$$\nAB=4\\,\\mathrm{cm}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nBC=2\\,\\mathrm{cm}\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAC &= AB + BC \\\\\n6 &= 4 + 2 \\\\\n6 &= 6.✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a true statement, so the three points $A$, $B$, and $C$ are indeed collinear.\n\n**Note:** The postulate works in both directions:\n\n- If the points $A$, $B$, and $C$ are collinear then $AC= AB + BC$.\n- If $AC= AB + BC$ then the points $A$, $B$, and $C$ are collinear." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139635 #edb/ref 1065 2168 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139635 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "0a460529-da55-5fcc-a207-e4ae6a47f12d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139635 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139636 #edb/ref 1064 "Given two of the three distances between three collinear points, we can calculate the third distance using the segment addition postulate.\n\nTo demonstrate, consider the collinear points $A$, $B$, and $C$ below.\n\n![](images/3b/3b1bc383e8d570f9dc33346e6f4ae04377e3c5c4c0d0e53abe07b0aacd9bb3e6.png)\n\nWe are given two of the distances:\n\n$$\nAB = 3cm, AC = 5cm\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the third distance, $BC$, using the segment addition postulate.\n\nFirst, we state the equation and substitute the known quantities $AB=3$ and $AC=5$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAC &= AB + BC \\\\\n5 &= 3 + BC\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for $BC$ by subtracting $3$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5 &= 3 + BC \\\\\nBC &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance between the points $B$ and $C$ is $2\\,\\textrm{cm}$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139636 #edb/ref 1065 2169 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139636 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "92286c39-6af9-5ff0-b2e9-fa44fec8f732" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139636 #edb/ref 1067 "Calculating the Distance Between Collinear Points" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
